1. What is a process?
A program is passive executable code stored in a file. A process is an instance of a program currently being executed.
A process includes:

its program code;
stack, heap and global data;
CPU state, such as the program counter and registers;
operating-system information stored in its Process Control Block (PCB):

process ID;
current process state;
scheduling information;
memory-management information;
open resources.


Multiple processes can therefore execute the same program while having different variables, stacks, registers and process IDs.

2. What does fork() do?
In a POSIX system,
pid_t p = fork();
creates one new child process.
Immediately after a successful call, there are two processes:

the original parent process;
the newly created child process.

Both continue execution at the instruction immediately following fork().
Conceptually, the child receives a copy of the parent’s state at the moment of the call:

program code;
stack, heap and global variables;
environment;
open file descriptors;
current position in the program.

Ordinary variables are subsequently independent. Changing a variable in the child does not change the parent’s corresponding variable.
In practice, the memory pages are usually initially shared using copy-on-write and copied only when one process modifies them. For the exam, however, you can reason as if each process has its own copy.

Return value
The processes distinguish themselves using the return value:



Result of fork()
Meaning




p &gt; 0
This is the parent; p is the child’s PID


p == 0
This is the child


p == -1
Creation failed; no child was created



A very common pattern is therefore:
pid_t p = fork();

if (p == 0) {
    // Executed by the child
} else if (p &gt; 0) {
    // Executed by the parent
} else {
    // Error handling
}
The exact positive PID is normally unknown unless it is provided.


Crucial distinction
fork() is called once, but it returns twice:

once in the parent, with a positive value;
once in the child, with zero.

int x = 1;

for (int i = 0; i &lt; 2; i++) {
    pid_t r = fork();                 // F
    if (r == 0) {
        x = x + i + 1;
        break;
    }
    x = x + 10;
}

if (x &lt; 10) {
    fork();                          // G
}

emit(x);

a) — 5 points
Draw the complete process tree, labeling every creation edge with the call site and, for call F, the loop iteration.
State:

the total number of processes created, including the original process;
the meaning of the return value of call F in its parent and child;
which processes execute call G.



b) — 2 points
Give the value output by each process and the total number of output operations. Is the order of these outputs uniquely determined?


c) — 3 points
Name the five states of the basic process-state model.
Give the state transition associated with each event:

A running process requests a blocking disk read.
That disk read completes.
A running process exhausts its CPU quantum.

Aufgabe 7
8 points
A process executes:
int value = 4;
pid_t child = fork();

if (child == 0) {
    value = 9;
    emit("child", value);
    exit(0);
}

waitpid(child);
emit("parent", value);
Assume:

fork() succeeds;
value is ordinary process-local memory;
waitpid(child) waits for that child’s termination and returns immediately if the child has already terminated;
emit produces one complete output record without buffering effects;
no signals interrupt the operations.


a) — 2 points
What does child contain in each process, and which process reaches waitpid?


b) — 2 points
Give the output records and their required ordering. Justify the value in the parent’s record.


c) — 2 points
Suppose the parent reaches waitpid before the child terminates.
Give the parent’s state transition when it waits and its state transition when the child’s termination makes it eligible to continue. Does the termination guarantee that the parent immediately executes its next instruction?


d) — 2 points
Consider a replacement using two threads in one process:
shared int value = 4

Worker:
    value = 9
    terminate

Main:
    start Worker
    join Worker
    emit("main", value)
Assume join returns only after the worker terminates and makes its writes visible.
What value does Main output, and why does the memory behavior differ from the fork() program?
